Criteria recorded before opening docs/design/value-types.md

Authority: docs/spec.md is normative; syntax.md fixes the intended keyword-free syntax and declared UpName variables; a1-front-end.md specifies staging. Related constraints read in literal-types.md (literal sets, known types, value restriction), records.md (variance, label parameters, codecs), meta.md (hygiene and checking order). Principles supplied by reviewer request apply: standalone language, no keywords or privileged libraries, sets of values, three unrelated uses, principal inference.

1. Give one precise set-of-values meaning to a lowercase type reference. Distinguish binding scheme, a fresh instance, monomorphic inference variables, known type, and singleton value. No runtime evaluation or hidden effects from type syntax. Test literals, annotated bindings, identity, empty lists, calls returning functions, and non-generalized values whose types later uses constrain.
2. Extraction must preserve variable sharing, variance, value restriction and rank-1 inference. Hard cases: id used twice; pairs of id; local parameter x reused in y:x; covariant producer and contravariant consumer arguments; invariant type arguments; absent lower bounds; unconstrained variables and Never; projection of a polymorphic component; a type referenced repeatedly through aliases versus directly.
3. Alias patterns need unambiguous declaration/lookup, arity, duplicate and scope rules. Test Schema(User, _) with User absent/present, all-UpName heads versus generic aliases, imported/qualified heads, nested nominal/tuple/function/alias heads, repeated new names, ignored polymorphic parts, and ordinary transparent aliases that erase or permute arguments. Equal types must not accidentally require inversion of a non-injective alias.
4. Dependency scheduling must terminate, preserve principal inference, be independent of source order at module level, and leave runtime initialization untouched. Test forward type-to-value chains; aliases through aliases; custom fields referring to values; cycles through inferred function results; recursive functions with written signatures; two otherwise independent values connected by a type reference; a value made non-generalized by a call; variance depending on a later value-dependent field.
5. Scope must match the ordinary language or explicitly justify an extension. Test local shadowing of a module, parameter annotations referring to earlier/later/self parameters, outer names in parameter annotations, local alias scope, exported aliases/private nominal types, fields versus module members, macro hygiene, and labels in written function types that are not bindings.
6. Every written-type restriction must still apply after substitution: multi-variable input unions, variable output intersections, variable RHS of difference, runtime type tests with unknown heads, environment service identity, host admissibility, label parameter restrictions. Aliases must not hide forbidden forms.
7. Plain libraries should demonstrate at least three unrelated uses (schema/codec output, parser errors or results, service/callback types). Check that examples do not require extracting an unstable input type, rank-2 schemes, reflection of private types or special casing Schema.
8. Implementation plan must identify syntax/HIR forms, binder identity, dependency walkers, solver phase and representation, variance pass, known-type and scheme substitution, export/interface and query consumers. Citations must really support the claimed behavior; planned extensions are not defects merely because absent today. Check parser migration (A3), macro phases and no new runtime work.

Report only concrete contradictions, unsafe acceptances, unjustified rejections or implementation gaps with a reproducer; distinguish run evidence, code inspection and inference. For each recommended change give its source/reason, a simpler rule if feasible, and evidence that could overturn the recommendation.
